WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.267
The eight lakh rupee monthly API bill.

2
00:00:03.567 --> 00:00:05.258
Finance wants it at two lakh.

3
00:00:05.258 --> 00:00:07.767
Here's how a strong engineer approaches it.

4
00:00:08.967 --> 00:00:10.300
Here's the situation.

5
00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:14.133
Fifty thousand conversations a day, around six turns each.

6
00:00:14.433 --> 00:00:20.567
Every turn sends a two thousand token system prompt plus context, all on one frontier model.

7
00:00:20.867 --> 00:00:22.900
Finance wants eight lakh down to two.

8
00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:25.833
First, attribute the cost.

9
00:00:25.833 --> 00:00:27.633
Estimate before optimizing.

10
00:00:27.933 --> 00:00:38.700
Three hundred thousand calls a day, each carrying prompt and context, adds up to about one point two billion input tokens every day, roughly thirty six billion a month.

11
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:41.847
Output is only around ninety million a day.

12
00:00:41.847 --> 00:00:43.900
This is an input-token problem.

13
00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:47.900
Now four levers, ordered by savings per effort.

14
00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:52.881
Prompt caching: put the stable prompt first, and repeats bill at a fraction.

15
00:00:52.881 --> 00:00:53.867
No quality risk.

16
00:00:54.167 --> 00:00:56.733
Model routing, the biggest structural saving.

17
00:00:57.033 --> 00:01:00.667
Cache answers, but never anything personalized across users.

18
00:01:00.967 --> 00:01:01.973
And trim context.

19
00:01:01.973 --> 00:01:05.700
Do you really need five chunks, or the full history every turn?

20
00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:08.267
Here's how routing works.

21
00:01:08.567 --> 00:01:11.500
Most support turns are simple, like where's my order.

22
00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:15.800
A cheap classifier sends them to a model roughly ten times cheaper.

23
00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:19.300
Only hard reasoning turns reach the frontier model.

24
00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:22.267
Now prove quality didn't drop.

25
00:01:22.567 --> 00:01:30.733
Run your golden set before and after each lever, and A/B the router on live traffic, with an LLM judge sampling two percent.

26
00:01:31.033 --> 00:01:34.076
Report one table: cost, latency, and quality.

27
00:01:34.076 --> 00:01:38.133
That table is the deliverable, not the savings number alone.

28
00:01:39.333 --> 00:01:42.129
The trap is jumping to self-hosting a model.

29
00:01:42.129 --> 00:01:44.733
It's weeks of work and operations burden.

30
00:01:45.033 --> 00:01:47.967
Caching and routing get most of the way in days.

31
00:01:49.167 --> 00:01:49.999
Let's recap.

32
00:01:49.999 --> 00:01:51.733
Attribute the cost first.

33
00:01:52.033 --> 00:01:55.067
Then cache, route, cache answers, and trim context.

34
00:01:55.367 --> 00:01:57.400
Prove it with a before and after table.

35
00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:00.065
And always the cheapest effort first.

36
00:02:00.065 --> 00:02:03.133
Next up: the agent that deleted production data.
